<!--BlockML Documentation — a guide to the Block-versus-instance distinction

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs.BlockDefinitionsAndInstances-->
<docs:BlockDefinitionsAndInstances xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:docs="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs"
  xmlns:dpage="org.blockml.bml.docgen.page"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation"
  xmlns:def="org.blockml.bml.definition"
  xmlns:lrule="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.lib.rule">

  <baseType>
    dpage:DocPage
  </baseType>
  <is>
    A guide to the Block-versus-instance distinction
  </is>
  <documentation>
    Scope: Block definition versus Block instance and the class/object analogy, grounded in
    org.blockml.bml.documentation.BlockAndInstance and org.blockml.bml.definition.BlockDefinition.
    Body content is authored in a04 from those sources.
  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="doc:BlockAndInstance" relation="Requires">
      Primary content source — Block as declarative class, instance as object, composition as containment.
    </see>
    <see type="def:BlockDefinition" relation="Requires">
      Meta-model shape of a Block definition document.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:MemberAuthoring" relation="SeeAlso">
      Definition-field versus instance-payload syntax that follows from this distinction.
    </see>
  </references>
  <properties>
    <title override="true">
      <value>Block Definitions and Instances</value>
    </title>
    <detailLevel override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.level.Introductory</value>
    </detailLevel>
    <pageType override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.pagetype.Concept</value>
    </pageType>
  </properties>
  <aggregations>
    <rules override="true">
      <composition>
        <lrule:CodeExamplePlacementRule />
        <lrule:CodeExplanationRule />
      </composition>
    </rules>
    <content override="true">
      <composition>
        <!--1. Lead-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Class and object, semantically">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text text="The closest familiar model for a Block versus a Block instance is a declarative semantic Class versus an Object." />
                <dpage:Text text="That is a semantic analogy, not a mandate that the compiler represent either one as a runtime object — internally, the compiler may store both as plain data." />
                <dpage:Text text="The parser nonetheless keeps the two apart: it always distinguishes a Block document, which defines a class, from a composition child, which is one occurrence of a type." />
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--2. Two contexts-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Two contexts, never mixed">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text text="A Block definition is the .bml file that describes a Block's shape and behaviour — its FQN, its members, and its composition schema." />
                <dpage:Text text="It has no runtime and no address of its own; it only describes what instances of that type may look like." />
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text text="A Block instance is one concrete occurrence placed inside composition, carrying its own member values." />
                <dpage:Text text="It is identified by a bml:id rather than by its type FQN, since many instances of the same type can exist side by side." />
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--3. The most common confusion-->
        <dpage:Section heading="The most common authoring confusion">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text text="The most common authoring mistake is wrapping an instance payload in a value or composition element — those two fields belong only to member declarations on a Block definition, never to a Block instance." />
                <dpage:Text text="On a Block instance, the Property element or attribute is already the value, and the Aggregation element already holds its children directly, so adding either wrapper is a category error, not a stylistic choice." />
                <dpage:Text text="The one exception is a member that is itself named value, such as on PrimitiveEntity — there, writing value on the instance is ordinary instance syntax for that member, not a definition wrapper." />
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--4. Minimal example-->
        <dpage:Section heading="A definition and one instance of it">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text text="Placing a Block's definition beside one instance of it makes the class/object analogy concrete: the definition declares a property, and an instance of it supplies a value directly." />
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
            <dpage:CodeBlock>
              <code><![CDATA[<!-- material is a Property declaration; its default is expressed through the definition-only value field -->
<acme:Wall xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:acme="com.acme.example"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:type="org.blockml.bml.type">

  <baseType>
    core:Block
  </baseType>
  <properties>
    <material type="type:Text">
      <value>Brick</value>
    </material>
  </properties>
</acme:Wall>]]></code>
            </dpage:CodeBlock>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text text="material's declaration on Wall's definition exists once, expressed through the value field of its PropertyDefinition — value is definition-only syntax and never appears on an instance." />
                <dpage:Text text="An instance of Wall placed in composition elsewhere would simply write material=&quot;Brick&quot; as a plain attribute, or as the material element's direct content — no value wrapper, because the Property on an instance is already the value." />
                <dpage:Text text="That is the class-declares, object-holds relationship the analogy describes: one declaration, many possible concrete occurrences." />
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--5. What to carry forward-->
        <dpage:Section heading="What to carry into the next pages">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text text="After this page, readers should be able to tell a Block definition from a Block instance in any piece of BML, and keep definition-only fields like value out of instance payloads." />
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <!--6. Continuation-->
        <dpage:Section heading="Continue with inheritance">
          <content>
            <dpage:Navigation>
              <title>Next</title>
              <items>
                <dpage:NavigationItem label="Inheritance" summary="Learn specialization via baseType.">
                  <target>docs:Inheritance</target>
                </dpage:NavigationItem>
              </items>
            </dpage:Navigation>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
      </composition>
    </content>
  </aggregations>
</docs:BlockDefinitionsAndInstances>
